{"name":"Bubbly Barracuda","hex":"#77ccff","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bubbly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Full of bubbles.","Cheerful, lively."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Champagne."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bubbly"},{"term":"barracuda","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any large marine fish of the genus Sphyraena that have elongated bodies, a projecting lower jaw, displaying prominent fang-shaped teeth, and are aggressive predators.","One who uses harsh or predatory means to compete."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/barracuda"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bubbly","blocks":[{"text":"\"Bubbly\" is the debut single of American singer-songwriter Colbie Caillat from her first album, Coco (2007). Written by Caillat and Jason Reeves and produced by Mikal Blue, the song was released as the album's lead single on May 15, 2007. \"Bubbly\" is Caillat's only song to peak within the top 10 of the US Billboard Hot 100; it remains her highest-charting song. Internationally, \"Bubbly\" reached number one in Australia, Brazil, and the Czech Republic. It also became a top-10 hit in several European countries, including Belgium, Germany, and Norway, as well as Japan and New Zealand. It is certified six-times platinum in the US and Australia as well as gold or higher in six additional countries."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f5/Bubbly_%28Colbie_Caillat%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bubbly","wikidata":"Q2552843"},{"title":"Barracuda","blocks":[{"text":"A barracuda is a large, predatory, ray-finned, saltwater fish of the genus Sphyraena, the only genus in the family Sphyraenidae, which was named by Constantine Samuel Rafinesque in 1815. It is found in tropical and subtropical oceans worldwide ranging from the eastern border of the Atlantic Ocean to the Red Sea, on its western border the Caribbean Sea, and in tropical areas of the Pacific Ocean. Barracudas reside near the top of the water and near coral reefs and sea grasses. Barracudas are often targeted by sport-fishing enthusiasts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Barracuda_laban.jpg/330px-Barracuda_laban.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barracuda","wikidata":"Q193921"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Great_Barracuda%2C_North_Caicos%2C_TC_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_357718805.jpg/500px-Great_Barracuda%2C_North_Caicos%2C_TC_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_357718805.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Great_Barracuda%2C_North_Caicos%2C_TC_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_357718805.jpg/1280px-Great_Barracuda%2C_North_Caicos%2C_TC_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_357718805.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Great_Barracuda%2C_North_Caicos%2C_TC_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_357718805.jpg/500px-Great_Barracuda%2C_North_Caicos%2C_TC_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_357718805.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Barracudas have a single large caniniform tooth at the tip of the lower jaw, which fits into a notch in the upper jaw[5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreat_Barracuda%2C_North_Caicos%2C_TC_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_357718805.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Great_Barracuda%2C_Buraco_das_Cabras%2C_Brasil_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_166323527.jpg/500px-Great_Barracuda%2C_Buraco_das_Cabras%2C_Brasil_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_166323527.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=